WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:09.620
This element is unique in the periodic table
 - element 117, Uus, because

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:14.469
it’s never been observed in any form at
all.

00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:18.160
On the 5th of April, which is just a few days
ago, there was a really,

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:24.650
really quite key paper accepted into a physics
journal. This essentially

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:31.590
told us that some folk in Russia in Dubna
had managed to make a new

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:36.129
element. Here on my computer screen let’s
bring up the periodic table.

00:00:36.129 --> 00:00:40.100
So this is the periodic table as we know it.
All of the groups, all of the

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:44.629
periods, right the way down to number 118.
There is only one element

00:00:44.629 --> 00:00:48.819
which isn’t coloured, ok, so it doesn’t
belong to an s, p, d or f block. It

00:00:48.819 --> 00:00:54.479
hasn’t been identified. And that’s element
number 117. So this paper

00:00:54.479 --> 00:00:56.440
which is published by the folk from the Joint
Institute For Nuclear

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:02.319
Research in Dubna claim to have produced a
small number of atoms of

00:01:02.319 --> 00:01:07.210
element number 117. No no, it’s really exciting
because it’s the

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:11.350
generation of new matter. It’s almost like
a chill goes up your spine

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:15.770
that’s like “wow, something new, something
really exciting.” It’s almost

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:20.520
akin to the announcement of Nobel Prizes,
a celebration of good science.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.380
So yeah it’s a really exciting time.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:28.140
The element 117 is a synthetic element. What
this means is that it’s not

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.549
stable or that it’s not found naturally
on the planet, ok. So all of the

00:01:31.549 --> 00:01:36.979
elements with an atomic number greater than
that of uranium are non-

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:41.149
stable, they all decay or they undergo radioactive
decay processes; and

00:01:41.149 --> 00:01:46.070
117 is one of these. Now if we were to study
the chemistry of any of

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:49.521
these higher or super-heavy elements, we actually
have to make them.

00:01:49.521 --> 00:01:52.549
We have to make them using linear accelerators,
and very fast

00:01:52.549 --> 00:01:57.530
accelerating technologies. Now you might remember
Martyn did a video

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:00.090
when he went to the linear accelerating facility
in Germany and he told

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:06.560
us a little bit about an analogy about how
you make these new atoms or

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.560
these new elements and his analogy was really
with snow. So he made a

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:16.360
snowball and he threw some snow, a snowball
at a tree or a target which

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.130
already had some snow on it, and the lump
of snow on the tree got

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:24.980
bigger and bigger and bigger and bigger. So
what these guys have done

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:29.030
is they’ve taken a target, which in this
case is berkelium. It’s a synthetic

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:32.650
material which was made in the United States.
It took a tremendous

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.780
amount of effort to make this material in
itself, but that’s a completely

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:39.819
different story. And they took this berkelium
which incidentally has a

00:02:39.819 --> 00:02:43.920
half-life of something in the order of 270
days I think, in fact no it’s 320

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:49.420
days the half-life of berkelium. So these
experiments had to be done

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.160
pretty quickly after the berkelium had been
made. This was then

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:59.150
transferred to the Russian group who formed
a target. So a target is a

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:05.739
bit like a CD or a disc, it’s nice and flat
and circular and it rotates inside a

00:03:05.739 --> 00:03:10.080
beam. And that beam is really what the accelerator
does, because it

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:15.590
takes ions and, in this case, in the Dubna
case in Russia, these are

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:18.409
calcium ions, so these are calcium-20 with
lots and lots of neutrons, so

00:03:18.409 --> 00:03:24.510
48-calcium, and it shoots a very fast beam
of these ions directly at the

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:30.079
target. Now they impact and hopefully they
undergo fusion and they

00:03:30.079 --> 00:03:33.860
generate the new atom or the new element.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:37.780
You might assume that these experiments might
take very long periods of

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:40.870
time and this is true, because the time taken
in Dubna was something in

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:46.269
the order of between 5 and 7 months to prepare
just 6 atoms of this new

00:03:46.269 --> 00:03:50.360
material. So a quite fantastic effort and
quite a fantastic research

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:53.780
program involving world-leading scientists
from the Oak Ridge National

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:56.489
Laboratories and also the Dubna Laboratories
in Russia.

00:03:56.489 --> 00:03:59.790
What’s going to happen with the name now
by the way?

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:03.650
Well, the name of the element, wow! That’s
a fantastic question isn’t it,

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:13.440
because it already has a Latin name: ununseptium
 – 117, ok. And this

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:18.010
really is a place-holder in the periodic table.
So the process now is that

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.450
somebody has to verify these results, and
once the results have been

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:23.889
verified, an open discussion will be held
and that will be moderated by

00:04:23.889 --> 00:04:28.440
IUPAC, International Union of Pure and Applied
Chemistry, in the same

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:33.030
way that a new name came up with copernicium
in the past. So, at the

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:37.770
moment, many people, especially those in Russia,
will start to use

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:42.241
Mendeleev’s naming convention for gaps in
the periodic table. And

00:04:42.241 --> 00:04:45.810
Mendeleev would have called this material:
eka-astatine, because

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:49.860
astatine is the element above it in the periodic
table. I’m really not sure

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.480
who will actually give the name to this material
because this was a true

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:53.480
collaboration between world-renowned scientists
in both the United

00:04:53.480 --> 00:05:00.889
States and also in Russia. So I dare say they’ll
have a ‘pow-wow’ round

00:05:00.889 --> 00:05:03.740
the table and they’ll come up with a very
nice name.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:08.530
This evidence or this kinetic evidence is
fantastic. Very, very good

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:13.419
supporting evidence for commonly held theories
of islands of stability

00:05:13.419 --> 00:05:17.520
where new elements may well be discovered
with much, much higher

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.509
atomic mass. And these would be over and above
the recognised or the

00:05:22.509 --> 00:05:26.360
accepted top value in our table of 118. The
new element 117 is an

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:30.139
interesting element and we can perhaps suggest
things about its

00:05:30.139 --> 00:05:35.194
chemistry by looking at the periodic table.
So if we look at the periodic

00:05:35.194 --> 00:05:38.550
table we can see that it sits quite happily
in a group which we know as

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:43.970
the halogens. So it sits below fluorine, chlorine,
bromine, iodine, and

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:51.919
astatine. So we know right away that it’s
going to want to form an anion.

00:05:51.919 --> 00:05:54.250
It’s going to want to take an extra electron
from an electron donor. But

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:58.270
what will the element look like? Well in its
bulk form I would guess,

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:01.440
looking at the periodic table, it would be
metal or a metalloid and it

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:04.860
would probably be very dark and lustrous.
So it would be shiny but quite

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:07.500
dark. But we have to make more than 6.

